Context-Switch Overhead Debugging
Answer
Given
Context Switches = 20,000/sec

Average Burst Time = 2 ms

Time Quantum = 1 ms

I/O Wait Frequency = Very High
Debug Analysis
The time quantum (1 ms) is smaller than the average CPU burst time (2 ms).
Threads are preempted before completing their execution.
Very frequent I/O operations cause threads to enter the waiting state and resume often.
This leads to a large number of context switches, increasing CPU overhead and reducing system performance.
Root Cause
Small Time Quantum
        +
High I/O Wait Frequency
        ↓
Frequent Context Switching
        ↓
High CPU Overhead
Optimizations

1. Increase the Time Quantum

Increase the time quantum (e.g., from 1 ms to 4 ms) so that threads can complete more work before being preempted.
This reduces the number of context switches.

2. Optimize I/O Operations

Use asynchronous I/O.
Buffer or batch I/O requests to reduce the frequency of blocking and waiting.
Result
Context Switches Reduced

CPU Overhead Reduced

Improved Throughput

Better Server Performance
